 i think it's a incredibly  natural  human instinct to protect our children  and i think sometimes  in things that there are questions or if  there's unknowns it's a little bit  easier for us to  take that risks with ourselves than it  is with our with our babies with our  children i think it's important to to  remember that there are  many many known risks to covet  infections the risks of vaccines are far  more theoretical kathy who is vaccinated  says she'd like to see more data from  the trials before coming to a decision  on vaccinating her eight-year-old  daughter i believe in science i believe  that the facts are there um i do  however wish that the data from the  trials was more  more available to the public and also  sort of in layman's terms as opposed to  being  hard data to process and really sort  through yourself  with the potential authorization of  pfizer's vaccine in younger children on  the horizon pediatricians across the  country are working to share information  about the vaccine with parents one  challenge they say is that the public  health message over the last year has  been that kids usually don't get very  sick from the virus dr gamble says there  are three main points that she tries to  hit during conversations with families  the first thing to remember is that we  know the vaccine works from studies in  adults we know that the vaccine helps  prevent disease and we also know that  vaccinated individuals that do catch  covid are far less likely to suffer from  severe complications or to die i have  every reason to believe that the vaccine  will be just as effective in kids the  second reason is that we know children  spread the virus that causes copin 19  disease even children that have no  symptoms can spread covid19 and the  vaccine helps decrease this spread the  third consideration is really thinking  about the broader impact that this  pandemic has had on kids mental health  and well-being every single day i'm  seeing the physical and emotional  manifestations of chronic stress on our  children pediatricians say a common  question they hear from parents is about  the possibility of unknown long-term  side effects from the vaccine because  it's new that's not a question that i  haven't heard before the pneumococcal  vaccine the vaccine to prevent  hemophilus influenza type b chickenpox  vaccine were all vaccines that i've seen  developed in my career and those  questions came up before and the answer  today is what it was then the studies  that we've done to look at this vaccine  has shown that it is safe and there's no  expectation of anything happening long  term with those other three vaccines  that has proven to be true the  population of children that i'm actually  immunizing now are the children of those  people that i immunize for the first  time with some of those uh particular  vaccines and so i can point to them when  i see that mother that i took care of as  a child and say you you came out okay  dr patterson says he's also worked to  ally specific concerns about fertility  myocarditis and pericarditis by sharing  data and research with families  continuing to help them understand  how science is uh paying attention to  side effects how we are uh capturing  potential problem areas and how we are  really making a difference in the  outcome that people are having those are  some of the ways to convince or  encourage those who take the wait and  see approach  that that now is the time should the fda  greenlight the vaccine for young  children they would get two injections  three weeks apart like adolescents and  adults but at a lower dosage after the  authorization in 5 to 11 year olds the  only group left awaiting vaccines will  be children as young as 6 months pfizer  has said that results from a study in  younger children ages 6 months to under  5 years could come towards the end of  2021
 

